          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Oresteia</strong><br>\nThe Oresteia is Aeschylus's only surviving trilogy and is considered his masterpiece. It consists of three plays: \"Agamemnon,\" \"The Libation Bearers,\" and \"The Eumenides.\" The trilogy tells the story of King Agamemnon's murder by his wife, Clytemnestra, and the subsequent revenge by their son, Orestes.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Innovations in Stagecraft</strong><br>\nApart from introducing a second actor, Aeschylus is credited with major contributions to the development of stagecraft. He made use of elaborate costumes, scenic design, and even mechanical devices to dramatize his plays. His use of masks, which were larger than life, added to the expressive power of the actors and their characters.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Religious and Cultural Themes</strong><br>\nAeschylus's work is heavily influenced by the religious and cultural beliefs of his time. His plays address the will of the gods, fate, and the nature of human suffering. They reflect the ancient Greeks' views on morality, justice, and the social order, all while entertaining and educating the audience.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
