          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Persian Wars Influence</strong><br>\nThe Persian Wars left a significant mark on Aeschylus's writing. He used his experience as a soldier to enrich his plays with realistic battle scenes and the sense of pride that characterized the Greek victory. His play \"The Persians\" remains a valuable historical source for understanding the perspective of the Greeks on this conflict.</p>\n\n<p><strong>A Legacy in Drama</strong><br>\nAeschylus's influence on drama extended beyond his lifetime and the borders of Greece. His work laid the groundwork for later playwrights like Sophocles and Euripides. The conventions and themes he established are still central to the dramatic arts and have shaped storytelling across the world.</p>\n\n<p><strong>His Death and Memoriam</strong><br>\nAeschylus died around 456/455 BC. According to legend, he was killed when an eagle dropped a tortoise on his head, mistaking his bald scalp for a rock suitable for shattering the shell. He left a profound legacy, and his epitaph, allegedly written by himself, commemorates his participation in the Battle of Marathon rather than his success as a playwright.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
